\begin{theorem}
For the bounded class $C_\theta$ of continuous locally uniformly ordinary
smooth middle-positive weights on a compact good smooth boundaryless analytic
surface, the genuine mixed products have conserved mass
$V=\int_X\theta^2$. With $\alpha_u=\theta+d'd''[u]$,
\[
 E(u)=\frac13\int_Xu(\theta^2+\theta\wedge\alpha_u+\alpha_u^2),
 \qquad \frac{d}{dt}E(u+t(v-u))=\int_X(v-u)\alpha_{u+t(v-u)}^2.
\]
The energy is concave and its tangent inequality holds for every bounded
competitor. Comparison holds for this entire bounded class.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}
On the quotient over $K=\mathbf C((s))$, $|s|=e^{-1/8}$, with periods
$(s^8,2),(3,s^8)$, retain the specified published foundations. For
right-continuous nonincreasing $0\le h\le1$, constant $c$ on $[0,1)$,
put $Z=\int_0^\infty h^3<\infty$ and
$u_h=u_0-\int_0^{R}h$. Its genuine radial nonpluripolar measure has mass two:
\[
 \mu_h=(2-c^2)\delta_o+\xi_*[-d(h^2)],\qquad
 E(u_h)=-\frac16-\frac Z3.
\]
Its dual over the entire bounded class is exactly
\[
 \sup_{v\in C_\theta}\left(E(v)-\int_Xv\,d\mu_h\right)
 =\frac16+\frac{2Z}{3}.
\]
The unbounded operator and uniqueness asserted here are restricted to the
radial domain. No full intrinsic all-direction finite-energy theory follows.
\end{theorem}
